Me: Thanks! Here’s my first real question: I need to create a folder structure in GitHub for organizing my SAGE project. What would you recommend?

Kevin: That is a fantastic first "real" question! Honestly, getting your folder structure right at the start is like cleaning your desk before a big study session—it just makes everything feel more manageable.
Since I'm not 100% sure if your SAGE project is using SageMath (the math software) or if it's an acronym for something specific in your course, I'll give you a "Gold Standard" structure that works for almost any programming or data science project.
Here is what I’d recommend for your GitHub repository:
code Text
/SAGE-Project
│
├── data/               # Keep your raw datasets or input files here
├── notebooks/          # If you're using SageMath/Jupyter notebooks
├── src/                # Your main source code (Python scripts, etc.)
├── docs/               # Your project report, instructions, or notes
├── tests/              # (Pro move!) Files to test if your code works
├── .gitignore          # A file to tell GitHub what NOT to upload
└── README.md           # The "Front Page" that explains your project
Why this works:
The README.md: This is the most important part! It’s like the cover of a book. It tells your professor (or future employers) what the project is about and how to run it.
Separation of Concerns: By keeping your code (src) separate from your data, you won't accidentally delete your data while messing with your code.
Cleanliness: It makes your GitHub profile look professional and organized.
Kevin's Pro-Tip: Don't worry about getting it perfect on Day 1. The cool thing about Git is that we can always move folders around later as the project grows!
Quick question for you: Is this project focusing on math/equations (like SageMath), or is it a software development project? I want to make sure I'm giving you the most relevant advice!
